 ### [Bounding Box and Camera Center Calculation](7641-bounding-box-and-camera-center-calculation.md)
-This variant computes the axis-aligned bounding box of a set of vertices and calculates an orthographic/perspective camera center position based on the max edge length and a 45-degree field of view [G]. It initializes min/max values, iterates through vertices, computes the size vector, and calculates the center as the midpoint of min/max [G]. The camera center Z coordinate is calculated using the formula:
-$$C_z = Z_{max} + \frac{E_{max}}{\tan(22.5^\circ)}$$
-where $C_z$ is the camera center Z coordinate, $Z_{max}$ is the maximum Z bound, and $E_{max}$ is the maximum X or Y edge length [G]. The result is stored in a `CenteredView` struct containing a transform matrix and camera center vector [G].
+This method computes the geometric center and bounding radius of a voxel grid or mesh to position cameras and define clipping planes for volume rendering. It differs from sibling methods by explicitly deriving camera parameters (such as position and field of view) from the scene's spatial extent, whereas siblings like [Bounding Cube Creation](4007-bounding-cube-creation.md) or [Volume Bounds Computation](5114-volume-bounds-computation.md) focus solely on spatial bounds without camera integration. It builds on [Mesh Bounding Box Calculation](3612-mesh-bounding-box-calculation.md) by using the computed AABB to derive a centered view configuration.
 
